A||N||8||Seanh||How badly did I screw up? (Front Springs)||||1||
Z||000000||Seanh||07-02-2001||01:52 PM||||Well, I just noticed this today, so its really not too late to fix it, but I already got the knuckel and ball joints in, so I'm not looking to forward to it. On the drivers side spring, I didn't get the top of the spring seated in the little rubber thingy up there. The end of the spring doesn't fall where it used to, obvious from the impression left by the old one. Is this a problem??? Please don't tell me I have to take all this crap...I mean suspension components...off again. Also, If I leave it, should I do the same thing to the passegner side so it won't sit slightly uneven?  ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Leadfoot||07-02-2001||02:39 PM||||O man! That's bad.  The only solution is to sell the car cheap!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Actually I had the same problem. I was able to re-seat it with a pipe/crowbar.  Since you don't want to get your paint scratched I'd probably only remove the lower ball joint, drop the lower arm and try to spin it.  However, I am not sure how critical it is to have it seat in the imprint.  I would think worst case would be your car will sit a little higher, but then again what do I know.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrfiero||07-02-2001||04:38 PM||||The really important part is that the bottom of the spring be seated properly ino the lower control arm.  The rubber cap on top is just to prevent squeaking, etc., so as long as it is on you should be good to go (assuming it is seated in  the lower control arm).<P>You can rotate the spring by grabbing it and pulling (it's not going to be easy, but it's possible).<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Seanh||07-02-2001||04:43 PM||||I'm pretty sure its seated correctly in the lower arm. I didn't notice any indentations or anything where it should go, but it looks correct. I already connected the ball joints and steering knuckel, so I wasn't looking forward to removeing those again, but I guess it doesn't look like I'll have to. Is there any particular way the spring goes into the lower arm? ||||reg||1
Z||000004||mrfiero||07-02-2001||04:54 PM||||There should be a notch on the control arm  where the end of the coil butts up against. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Seanh||07-02-2001||05:19 PM||||OK, I see it now. The end of the spring is about 1/2" away from the notch. Is that ok, or should I remove and reinstall the spring. I just seperated the lower ball joint, and tried turning the spring, but it doesn't move for nothin. I would think that the spring would be fine like it is, maybe it might even work its way into the seats. Just luckly that the spring isn't going over the notches, it is sitting behind them a little. What so I do?||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||07-02-2001||07:45 PM||||You only need to seperate the lower joint to fix this. Should not take more then 20-30 minutes. I'd put it back where it belongs.||||reg||1
Z||000007||lowCG||07-03-2001||10:57 PM||||As long as the rubber pad isn't squished out from under the spring anywhere,a slight rotation won't hurt a thing.<BR>The front suspension will take approximately a week to settle fully anyways,so you can't bee too critical about the ride height right after assembling.<BR>I'd say better to not have the risk of messing up the rubber on the balljoint than to have to try and do it over again unless it's really necessary.||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||07-03-2001||11:48 PM||||What mess up the ball joint?<P>A fat bolt with nut and a section of pipe that fits over the joint stem. (If posible, Grind a pit in the bolt head to help keep it from slipping.) Loosen the castle nut but leave it on the stem and use it as a base to push against. Hold the "fat" bolt head still and turn its nut against the pipe. no joint damage at all and a bit easyer to make than the ones in the books. All you need is pipe, a saw, and a suitable nut and bolt.<P>You can do the whole thing in minutes.||||reg||1
